y record low","records":"Fort Lauderdale has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Fort Lauderdale's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1984.","rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 80\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>80\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 81\u00b0F, and the record is 87\u00b0F (1922, 1936, 1948, 1992). The low of <strong>67\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Fort Lauderdale has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Fort Lauderdale's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Fort Lauderdale has set <strong class=\"sig\">9 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 89 that chance alone would give, and <strong>71 record highs</strong>, close to the 88 that chance alone would give. The 1920s hold the most record highs of any decade (72). Fort Lauderdale's all-time high is 100\u00b0F, on August 4, 1944.","t_chg":"Fort Lauderdale's nights have warmed. Its days have warmed since 1984.","p_chg":"Across 94 complete years: Fort Lauderdale's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.60\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.52 to +0.68), about +6.6\u00b0F since 1914. Fort Lauderdale's average high shows no clear change: \u22120.05\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.11 to +0.02) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1984, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.97\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.74 to +1.20) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.30\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.12 to +0.48). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 11 of 12 months. Highs rose in none; January and September fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Fort Lauderdale's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.13 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 62.8 inches a year. The wettest year was 2023 (113.92 in); the driest, 1961 (35.54 in). Fort Lauderdale almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 5 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}